text:
priya: Heads up — a customer hit a weird runtime mismatch when using our SDK bundled with Vite: runtime throws 'Response.prototype.text is not a function' inside our SDK network layer. Repro shows vite + node-polyfills plugin auto-injects a fetch shim.

max: Ugh, Vite's polyfilling can be sneaky. Some plugins shim fetch/Request by adding a different implementation into the bundle (not the platform's global), so you get two different Request/Response constructors at runtime. That breaks instanceof checks and prototype methods.

lee: I saw similar in a monorepo: the bundler replaced globalThis.fetch but the app still used platform Request from edge (Cloudflare) so methods missing. Two pragmatic fixes: 1) avoid global polyfills when targeting edge/runtime that already supplies fetch; 2) make the SDK tolerate different fetch impls by duck-typing rather than instanceof.

priya: Duck-typing sounds good. Any sample for a robust check?

taylor: We added a tiny util in the SDK: `isWebFetchCompatible(obj)` that checks for minimal web fetch shape instead of relying on constructor identity. Example we used in the client bootstrap:

```js
function isWebFetchCompatible(f) {
  if (!f) return false;
  try {
    return typeof f === 'function' &&
      typeof Request === 'function' &&
      typeof Response === 'function' &&
      // quick smoke test: can construct Request-like object
      (typeof new Request("/", { method: "GET" }).clone === 'function');
  } catch (e) {
    return false;
  }
}

export async function resolveFetch(optFetch) {
  if (optFetch && isWebFetchCompatible(optFetch)) return optFetch;
  if (isWebFetchCompatible(globalThis.fetch)) return globalThis.fetch;
  try {
    const undici = await import('undici');
    if (isWebFetchCompatible(undici.fetch)) return undici.fetch;
  } catch (e) {
    // no undici available
  }
  throw new Error('No web-compatible fetch found. Pass a fetch in ClientOptions.');
}
```

max: That approach avoids instanceof traps and also prevents accidental global polyfill collisions. We also added a small README note for Vite: disable node polyfills or add an explicit alias to keep the platform fetch. For example, in vite.config.ts set `define: { 'globalThis.fetch': undefined }` or remove the polyfill plugin for edge builds.

lee: For TypeScript, downstream consumers had duplicate type errors when both lib.dom and undici types were present. To reduce friction we export a tiny type alias in the SDK: `export type BasicFetch = (info: RequestInfo, init?: RequestInit) => Promise<Response>;` and use that in public options. That way consumers on edge (dom) or node + undici both satisfy it without pulling undici types into our .d.ts.

priya: Nice. Anything to add about CJS/ESM or old bundlers rewriting conditionals?

taylor: Yup — older bundlers sometimes rewrite dynamic import calls which breaks our runtime selection. Recommendation: document two patterns in README: 1) runtime detection (resolveFetch) for modern stacks, 2) explicit injection for classic builds: `const client = new Client({ fetch: globalThis.fetch ?? require('undici').fetch })` (note: require only in CJS). Also include a tiny CJS example in sample-apps.

max: One more gotcha: some apps use `node-polyfills` that import `whatwg-fetch` style globals which implement fetch but don't implement streaming Response bodies. If your app depends on streaming, mention that in the FAQ and require a more complete polyfill (undici or node 18+).

priya: We'll add a short onboarding checklist in the SDK README: 1) if deploying to edge, do not include node polyfills; 2) prefer SDK resolveFetch helper; 3) if you need CJS compatibility, use the CJS sample consumer; 4) types: prefer BasicFetch alias.

ci-bot: Created issue #1290 "docs: Vite/polyfill guidance + robust fetch resolver + sample-apps CJS example" and opened PR #1301 with the resolveFetch util + README snippets. CI: running unit + smoke tests. :shipit:
